# Degree ψ(d) and generation at every d ∣ M

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.package_of_socket` (`e3f1941e-e249-507b-8bea-51cafb5aa8cf`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `9994796b-2f01-51ab-adad-3712b292b0f4`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field and $M$ a nonzero natural number, and suppose $\zeta \in K^\times$ is such that
its image in $K$ is a primitive $M$-th root of unity. Write $j =$ `jqModC K` for the Laurent series
$q^{-1}\cdot(E_4^3\eta^{-24})$ over $K$, that is, the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant with its
integer coefficients read in $K$, and for $N \neq 0$ write $j(q^N) =$ `jqNModC K N` for its image
under the ring homomorphism of $K((q))$ multiplying all exponents by $N$. Assume that for every
prime $p$ dividing $M$ the series $j(q^p)$ does not lie in the intermediate field $K(j)$ generated
by $j$ over $K$ inside $K((q))$. Then for every nonzero divisor $d$ of $M$ two things hold: first,
the $K(j)$-dimension of $K(j)\bigl(j(q^d)\bigr)$ equals $\psi(d) = \sum_{d' \mid d,\ d' \text{
squarefree}} d/d'$, the Dedekind psi function; second, the field $K\bigl(j, j(q^d)\bigr)$ coincides
with the field generated over $K$ by all the expansions $j(q^{d'})$ for nonzero divisors $d'$ of
$d$.

This is the assembly, over a field containing a primitive $M$-th root of unity, of the tower of
$q$-expansion models of the function fields of the modular curves $X_0(d)$: the degrees
$[K(j)(j(q^d)):K(j)] = \psi(d)$ and the generation of $K(j,j(q^d))$ by the divisor expansions are
obtained simultaneously for all $d \mid M$ from the prime-level non-membership hypothesis. It is the
source of the unconditional characteristic-zero degree formula and of its version under the same
hypotheses on the base field.

